This page lists the commands of `@micacms/editor` (the local editor) and of the self-hosted image. A command or option without a version exists since 0.2.0.

## The local editor

Install the editor in the project with `npm install --save-dev @micacms/editor`, then run each command with `npx mica <command>`. Before it is installed, `npx @micacms/editor <command>` does the same. `npx mica --help` shows the same list.

A path without an option is always the content root. Each command takes only its own options, and refuses the others with exit code 64.

The content root is the path that you give, or `MICA_CONTENT_ROOT`, or the directory with a `.mica/schema.json`, or `./content`, in this order. Mica looks for `.mica/schema.json` at most three levels below the current directory, and not in `node_modules` or a hidden directory. If it finds more than one, give the path.

### start

```bash
npx mica start [content dir]
```

Opens the editor on this machine. It has no sign-in, and it listens only on loopback.

| Option | Default | Effect |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `-c`, `--content <dir>` | Found by its `.mica/schema.json` | The content root. A path without a flag does the same. |
| `-p`, `--port <number>` | `PORT`, else `6422` | The port to listen on. |
| `--host <address>` | `HOST`, else `127.0.0.1` | The address to bind. |
| `--open`, `--no-open` | open in an interactive terminal, not in CI | Opens the browser, or does not. |
| `-h`, `--help` | | Shows the help. |
| `-v`, `--version` | | Shows the version. |

`start` stops with exit code 64 when the content directory does not exist, and with 78 when `.mica/schema.json` is missing. Run `npx astro sync` to write the model.

From 0.10.0, `start` also serves the tools for coding agents at `http://localhost:<port>/mcp`. See [Edit with an agent](/docs/agents/).

From 0.11.0:

- `start` stops with exit code 78 when the content root stores files with Git LFS and Git LFS is not installed or not set up. The message says what to do. See [Large files with Git LFS](/docs/git-lfs/).
- When `astro dev` runs, the editor shows **Preview** beside an entry. See [Preview changes](/docs/previews/).
- When the content is in a Git submodule on a detached HEAD, the banner names the command that checks out the branch, such as `git -C content switch main`, and Publish waits until the branch is checked out. See [Content in its own repository](/docs/content-in-its-own-repository/).

### init

Added in 0.5.0.

```bash
npx mica init
```

Adds Mica to the Astro project in the current directory. It writes a starter model in `src/mica.ts` and the collections in `src/content.config.ts`. Then it tells you which scripts (`mica`, `check`) to set in `package.json`. It never overwrites a file. It takes no options.

### check

Added in 0.5.0.

```bash
npx mica check [content dir]
```

Checks every entry against the model: fields that do not validate, missing media, relations to entries that do not exist, and files that Mica does not read. For a relation to a [reference set](/docs/reference-data/), it checks each id against the index in `.mica/references/`, and it names a set that has no index (0.11.0). It prints each problem. It exits with 0 when there is no error, 1 when there is one, and 78 when it cannot read the model.

| Option | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `-c`, `--content <dir>` | The content root. A path without a flag does the same. |

Use it in CI as `npm run check` or `npx --no mica check`: when `@micacms/editor` is missing, for example because CI installs no development dependencies, a plain `npx mica` can download and run an unrelated package called `mica`.

### import

Added in 0.6.0.

```bash
npx mica import [content dir] [options]
```

Moves Astro content collections into Mica, from Astro's data store. Without `--collection`, it imports every collection that the model defines. It exits with 78 when it cannot read the model. See [Add Mica to an existing site](/docs/existing-site/).

| Option | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `-c`, `--content <dir>` | The content root. A path without a flag does the same. |
| `--collection <name>` | Imports only this collection. Use it more than once. |
| `--from <file>` | Astro's data store. Default: `node_modules/.astro/data-store.json`, else `.astro/data-store.json`. |
| `--map <c.field=key>` | Takes a Mica field from another key. `@body` is the Markdown body. Use it more than once. |
| `--order <c=field>` | The first order of a sortable collection, by a field. |

### mcp

Added in 0.10.0.

```bash
npx mica mcp [content dir] [options]
```

Serves the tools for coding agents over stdio, without the editor. The agent starts this command itself: put it in the agent's MCP configuration. It writes its log to stderr. When you start it in a terminal yourself, it shows the configuration to give to your agent. `start` shows the same for its `/mcp` address. See [Edit with an agent](/docs/agents/).

| Option | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `-c`, `--content <dir>` | The content root. A path without a flag does the same. |
| `--allow-publish` | Offers the `publish` tool too. Without it, the agent saves drafts, and you publish them. `MICA_MCP_PUBLISH=true` does the same. |

`mcp` stops with the same exit codes as `start` when there is no content directory, no model, or no Git LFS for content that needs it.

### Exit codes

| Code | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `0` | Done. `check` found no error. |
| `1` | `check` found an error or no model, `import` failed, or `start` could not start, for example because the port is in use. |
| `64` | Wrong use: an unknown command or option, a missing value, no content directory, or `init` cannot run here. |
| `78` | Configuration: `start` found no content model, a model that it cannot read, a setting that the local editor does not allow, such as a `HOST` that is not on this computer, or content that needs Git LFS when Git LFS is not set up. |

## The self-hosted image

Without a command, or with `serve`, the image serves the editor. `check` tests the configuration, and `compact` makes the clone small again. Another command exits with 64.

### check

Added in 0.5.0.

```bash
docker compose run --rm mica check
```

Tests the configuration in a temporary clone, while the server can run:

- the configuration loads;
- the identity provider answers discovery, with the configured issuer. The line also shows the redirect URI to register;
- the token can read the branch;
- the token may push (a dry run, which cannot see branch protection or server hooks);
- the content root, and its model at `.mica/schema.json` loads;
- Git LFS: the line says when the content root does not use it, and otherwise that the image has Git LFS. It cannot prove write access to the LFS storage of the host: a dry run sends no request, and the first publication shows a refusal (0.11.0);
- the data volume is writable and has a layout that this version knows. If there is a clone, the line shows its head, the number of drafts, a commit that is not pushed, and work that the next start finishes.

It prints one line for each check, `ok` or `FAIL`, and exits with 0 when all pass, 1 when a check fails, and 78 when the configuration does not load. The settings are in [Configuration](/docs/configuration/). The endpoints are in [Health endpoints](/docs/reference-health/).

### compact

```bash
docker compose stop mica
docker compose run --rm mica compact
docker compose start mica
```

Makes a new clone on the data volume, with only the current files. The clone grows with each media change on the website, and a new clone is often much smaller. With Git LFS, it also drops the old versions of the large files from the store. It refuses a clone with drafts or with a publication that is not pushed yet: start Mica, publish or discard them, and stop it again. While another Mica uses the volume, it waits up to 45 seconds, then refuses. It exits with 0 when it compacted the clone or found none, 1 when the clone holds work, and 78 when the configuration does not load or another Mica uses the volume.
